{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Information.ObstructionVerdictCap","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Information.ObstructionVerdictCap","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Logic.Basic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.legs_independent_of_counter_model","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.legs_independent_of_counter_model","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L151-L158","doc":"**Restated as the corpus's disposition.** Given the same two facts, the legs\nare *not* forced together: the collision hypothesis fails, so `Collatz` and\n`PNeqNP` may both hold. This is the honest reading of \"one class, several\nrestrictions\" — the class is shared, the verdicts are not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.single_verdict_refuted_by_counter_model","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.single_verdict_refuted_by_counter_model","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L138-L149","doc":"**The single-verdict hypothesis is refuted by the corpus's own arithmetic.**\nFeed it the two facts the corpus proves at `χ(A) = -6` — the Collatz-side\nlevel-2 restriction **vanishes** (`2 ∣ -6`, so the `[2]_q` divisor is\nremovable), while the resolution-tree restriction does **not** (`q₀` strictly\nnon-torsion, so no finite Kummer level applies) — and it is inconsistent.\n\nHence no collision result above applies to the corpus, and the shared class\ntransfers nothing between legs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.collatz_iff_not_pnp_of_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.collatz_iff_not_pnp_of_single","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L125-L134","doc":"**Under the single-verdict hypothesis, q-Collatz and `P ≠ NP` become\nnegations.** The Collatz leg is faithful to *vanishing*, the P-vs-NP leg to\n*non-vanishing*; one verdict makes the two conjectures incompatible. That\nthis conclusion is absurd is a mark against the hypothesis, not against\neither conjecture. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.collatz_iff_nsRegular_of_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.collatz_iff_nsRegular_of_single","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L116-L123","doc":"**Under the single-verdict hypothesis, q-Collatz and q-NS regularity\ncollide.** Both legs sit on the vanishing side, so one verdict identifies\nthem: two problems the corpus treats as independent would become one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.SingleVerdict","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.SingleVerdict","kind":"def","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L109-L114","doc":"**The single-verdict hypothesis.** The Collatz, Navier–Stokes and\nresolution-tree restrictions of `κ₀` agree: the first two vanish together,\nand the third's vanishing is the same event. This is what \"one class, one\nverdict\" would require. It is **not** a corpus claim. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.opposite_polarity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.opposite_polarity","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L94-L99","doc":"**Opposite polarity.** A predicate faithful to a verdict and a predicate\nfaithful to its negation are negations of one another. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.verdict_cap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.verdict_cap","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ObstructionVerdictCap.lean#L86-L92","doc":"**Verdict cap.** If one verdict `v` is faithful to two predicates, those\npredicates are equivalent. This is the whole content of \"one scalar cannot\nanswer two questions differently\" — and it is `Iff` algebra, which is the\npoint: the mathematics is trivial, so all the weight sits on whether the\nhypotheses hold. Per the counter-model below, they do not. ","signatureChunk":0}]}